          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does asthma guidance describe as the stepwise approach, and what changed about reliever choice?",
          "answer": "The retrieved guidance covers the main direction of change, but not the full stepwise ladder or the strength of the evidence. My search for evidence strength failed, so I can't grade it.\n\n**The central change**\n\nThe asthma guidance in the retrieved material (a global respiratory strategy report and national asthma panel guidance) says that treating asthma with a short-acting bronchodilator alone is no longer appropriate at any severity. Adults and adolescents with asthma should receive a regimen that contains an inhaled corticosteroid. The stated reason is that reliever-only treatment leaves the underlying inflammation untreated and is linked to severe flare-ups, even in people with infrequent symptoms. The guidance also treats heavy reliever use, such as several canisters a year, as a marker of risk in its own right.\n\n**The stepwise structure**\n\nThe guidance arranges treatment in steps. The step is chosen from current symptom control and flare-up history, and it is reviewed over time rather than fixed. Review is advised one to three months after starting or changing treatment, then every three to twelve months, and within one week after a flare-up. The retrieved material does not include the detailed content of each step, so I can't set those out.\n\n**Where authorities differ**\n\nThe preferred reliever in mild asthma is a point of disagreement:\n- Global strategy guidance describes an inhaled corticosteroid and formoterol combination as the preferred reliever across steps.\n- Some national panel guidance describes a short-acting bronchodilator reliever, used alongside a regular controller, as still appropriate in mild disease.\n\nI can't say which position is better supported. Both are published positions, and the guidance itself doesn't settle the question.\n\n**Strength of evidence**\n\nMy attempt to retrieve the evidence behind these recommendations failed. So I can't tell you how strong the trials are, what outcomes they measured, or how certain the experts were. Please don't read the recommendations above as settled by strong evidence unless you check that yourself.\n\n**Next steps**\n\nIf you're asking because of your own asthma or someone else's, a clinician can match the step and reliever choice to the specific situation. If you want the evidence behind these recommendations, the original guideline documents list their supporting studies.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
